In this lesson we'll look at how three simple ratios connect the sides of a right‑angled triangle to one of its smaller angles. Every right triangle has a longest side called the hypotenuse, which sits opposite the right angle. The other two sides are easy to name once you pick an angle to focus on. The side directly across from that angle is called the opposite, and the side right next to it, not the hypotenuse, is called the adjacent. The three ratios are sine, cosine, and tangent, and each one pairs up two of those sides. Sine matches opposite over hypotenuse, cosine matches adjacent over hypotenuse, and tangent matches opposite over adjacent. The trick is to choose the one that includes the side you already know and the side you're trying to find. Once you do, just plug the numbers in and use your calculator to get the answer. Now let's see it in action.